<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Melus of Bari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1020</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Melus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> nobleman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> who led revolts against Byzantine rule in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. After a failed rising in 1009, he returned in 1017 with Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> to fight in the region, but was crushed by the catepan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-boioannes">Basil Boioannes</a> at Cannae in 1018. He died in exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> in 1020.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/melus-of-bari.jpg" alt="Melus of Bari" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: “Henricus [Public domain]” · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cape_of_Melus_of_Bari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Melus, also called Milus or Meles, came from the Apulian town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a> and sought to carve an autonomous territory for himself out of the Byzantine catapanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In 1009 he and his brother-in-law Dattus rebelled and quickly took Bari, and in 1010 they captured Ascoli and Troia. The new catapan, Basil Mesardonites, raised a large army, and Bari fell on 11 June 1011. Melus fled to Prince Guaimar III of Salerno, while Dattus found refuge at the abbey of Montecassino, which at the urging of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-benedict-viii">Pope Benedict VIII</a> gave him a fortified tower on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garigliano">Garigliano</a>. Melus' family was captured and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to the Norman chronicler William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, Melus went in 1016 to the shrine of Saint Michael at Monte Gargano and there asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rainulf-drengot">Rainulf Drengot</a> and a band of Norman exiles to join his rebellion, promising easy victory and rich spoils. In 1017 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> joined his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> forces at Capua and marched into Apulia. In May they won an encounter on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fortore">Fortore</a> against troops of the catapan Kontoleon Tornikios, and by September they held all the land between the Fortore and Trani.</p><p>The new catapan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-boioannes">Basil Boioannes</a>, gathered a large force that included a contingent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a> sent by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a>. He met the rebels on the Ofanto at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> had defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 216 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> lost their leader Gilbert, Melus fled to the lands of the Papal States and Dattus returned to his tower.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Melus wandered through southern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> before reaching the court of Emperor Henry II at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>. Henry honoured him with the empty title of Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, but Melus died soon after Pope Benedict came to Bamberg at Eastertide to discuss a response to the Byzantine victories. The emperor gave him a lavish funeral and an ornate tomb in the new Bamberg Cathedral. His son Argyrus, after returning from imprisonment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, continued the struggle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> independence in Apulia. Melus' own ambition had inadvertently begun the Norman presence in southern Italy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Melus of Bari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Local revolts were frequent: <b>Melus of Bari</b> rebelled in 1009 and again in 1017 with Norman mercenaries, but the catepan Basil Boioannes crushed them at Cannae in 1018 and founded new towns such as Troia.</blockquote><a href="/state/catepanate-of-italy">Catepanate of Italy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.13,16.87&amp;year=1010" aria-label="Bari on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4962 696 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Melus of Bari"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5412 966h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5412 966h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.13,16.87&amp;year=1010">What was here in 1010? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
